Points $A$, $B$, $Q$, $D$, and $C$ lie on the circle shown and the measures of arcs $BQ$ and $QD$ are $42^\circ$ and $38^\circ$, respectively.  Find the sum of the measures of angles $P$ and $Q$, in degrees.

[asy]
import graph;

unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, P, Q;

A = dir(160);
B = dir(45);
C = dir(190);
D = dir(-30);
P = extension(A,B,C,D);
Q = dir(0);

draw(Circle((0,0),1));
draw(B--P--D);
draw(A--Q--C);

label("$A$", A, NW);
label("$B$", B, NE);
label("$C$", C, SW);
label("$D$", D, SE);
label("$P$", P, W);
label("$Q$", Q, E);
[/asy]

One focus of the ellipse $\frac{x^2}{2} + y^2 = 1$ is at $F = (1,0).$  There exists a point $P = (p,0),$ where $p > 0,$ such that for any chord $\overline{AB}$ that passes through $F,$ angles $\angle APF$ and $\angle BPF$ are equal.  Find $p.$

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B, F, P;
path ell = xscale(sqrt(2))*Circle((0,0),1);

F = (1,0);
A = (sqrt(2)*Cos(80),Sin(80));
B = intersectionpoint(interp(A,F,0.1)--interp(A,F,5),ell);
P = (2,0);

draw(ell);
draw(A--B);
draw(A--P--B);
draw(F--P);

dot("$A$", A, N);
dot("$B$", B, SE);
dot("$F$", F, SW);
dot("$P$", P, E);
[/asy]
